.TH CCOL_MEMPOOL_CREATE_FROM_PREALLOCATED_BUFFER 3 "2026-08-28" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer \- create pool from user buffer
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cmempool.h>

ccol_mempool *ccol_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer(
    void *buffer,
    size_t buf_size,
    size_t elem_size,
    bool fallback_to_dynamic_memory,
    bool single_threaded,
    ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mmgmt_procs,
    char **err);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates a memory pool on top of a buffer that the caller provides, so
the pool takes no memory from the heap for its entries. It is useful on
embedded systems, and anywhere you don't want a heap allocation.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
buffer                     Pointer to the preallocated buffer
buf_size                   Size of the buffer in bytes
elem_size                  Size of each element in bytes. It must be
                          > 0. If it is smaller than sizeof(uintptr_t),
                          it is rounded up, the same way as in
                          ccol_mempool_create(). The maximum is
                          SIZE_MAX less the stride rounding
fallback_to_dynamic_memory If true, allocate from the heap when the pool
                          is empty
single_threaded           If true, take no lock
mmgmt_procs               Custom memory management, or NULL
err                       Optional pointer that receives an error string
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns a pointer to the new memory pool on success
Returns NULL on failure
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The element count is buf_size / (stride + 1), and an entry carries no
  header. The stride is elem_size rounded up to 16 bytes and then up to
  a power of two unless the library was built with
  -DCCOL_MEMPOOL_COMPACT_LAYOUT=1, so every element of the pool, not
  only the first one, is aligned for any object type. The extra byte
  for each element is its status byte, which lives in the same buffer
  after the entries. CCOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_MEMPOOL_BUFFER sizes its
  buffer with the same arithmetic, so use it to get the size right
  instead of writing the formula out a second time
ccol_mempool_destroy() does not free the buffer; its lifetime is the
  caller's business
The pool struct itself comes from mmgmt_procs
The pool calls the functions of mmgmt_procs for its dynamic fallback
entries, and for its own bookkeeping of them, in the middle of an
update of its own state and with its lock held when it has one. Those
functions must not call back into the same pool: with a lock the call
deadlocks, and without one it corrupts that state. An allocator built
on a pool must draw from a different pool
Returns NULL with an error if elem_size is zero
A nonzero elem_size below sizeof(uintptr_t) is rounded up, as in
  ccol_mempool_create()
Returns NULL with an error if elem_size would overflow size_t after
the stride rounding
Returns NULL with an error if the buffer is not aligned to at least 16
  bytes. Every entry of the pool is aligned to 16 bytes and entry 0
  sits at the address of the buffer, so the buffer must have that
  alignment. A buffer declared with
  CCOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_MEMPOOL_BUFFER already has it
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR CCOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_MEMPOOL_BUFFER (3),
.BR ccol_mempool_create (3),
.BR ccol_mempool_destroy (3)
